#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115110 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "46e2f515-2220-585d-927e-7f8a91af7bcb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5800" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=\\dfrac{1}{9}(x-3)(x+2)(x+3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}({\\color{blue}{x}}-3)({\\color{red}{x}}+2)(x+3) = \\dfrac 1 9\\cdot {\\color{blue}{x}}\\cdot {\\color{red}{x}}\\cdot x +\\cdots = \\dfrac 1 9 x^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=3,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{9}(0-3)(0+2)(0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(2)(3)}{9} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-2,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/71/7164fec4ddfa7b68489775b7feef639b61846188fc603186fe115f492dec606d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115111 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5914ea8b-a7f5-5fe0-8060-34cb47f5d401" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "3723bac3-ba0f-5c81-a59f-cf26836d7671" :question/math-academy-id "e-5801" :question/problem "Given that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x)=x^3-x^2-4x+4,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "We are told that $x=2$ is a root of $f(x),$ which means that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x).$ So, to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x-2)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -4 & 4 \\\\  &  & 2 & 2 & -4 \\\\  & 1 & 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)({x}^{2}+x-2) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x-1)(x+2),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other two roots of $f(x)$ are $x=1$ and $x=-2.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0-1)(0+2) \\\\  & =(-2)(-1)(2) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,1,2$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=4,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/77/77ab55ab6a105fd075d27e20286312d2c32118b113c7f0517deee9dbf3a16cff.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115113 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38c7027c-8dc4-5d3a-9a96-5d33d3b8c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "11960e10-03d2-57d2-bff1-d5d4285f4e98" :question/math-academy-id "e-5803" :question/problem "Graph the curve corresponding to the equation $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{2}(x+5)(1-x)(x+2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the $x^3$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}({\\color{blue}{x}}+5)(1-{\\color{red}{x}})(x+2) = \\dfrac 1 2\\cdot {\\color{blue}{x}}\\cdot (-{\\color{red}{x}})\\cdot x +\\cdots = -\\dfrac 1 2 x^3+\\cdots\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-5,$ $x=1,$ and $x=-2.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0+5)(1-0)(0+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{(5)(1)(2)}{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-5,-2,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=5,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c64b2285c626881d766811353184d2ecda06b2eaf2694b7d6b51db257115db9.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115114 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e03f532e-bf46-5933-baf3-b59e42b7cc67" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "d73c8c81-0fc1-5722-a134-65664b66b3d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5804" :question/problem "Given that $x=-1$ is a root of $f(x)=-x^3+4x^2-x-6,$ graph the curve $y=f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-1$ is a root of $f(x),$ this means that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ and therefore, we can begin factoring $f(x)$ by dividing it by $(x+1).$ We use synthetic division, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4 & -1 & -6 \\\\  &  & 1 & -5 & 6 \\\\  & -1 & 5 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)(-{x}^{2}+5x-6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)({x}^{2}-5x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)(x-2)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,2,3.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0-2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-(1)(-2)(-3) \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an ${\\textsf{S}}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-1,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-6,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/ac/acf7dc9cc405f9ae632df5a437d51c4175331ac59b6221ed9f0586c4c6b47446.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554600 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5914ea8b-a7f5-5fe0-8060-34cb47f5d401" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24c0dc1e-2f85-5424-80ef-3dcc1ab214bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40ab547f0afaa790fa659e6f4bdfdfae56b68c9b785047df165196cd483d3a52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553934} {:answer/id #uuid "4d71d480-77ad-564e-b1d3-b96ecf559a25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/af/af6d7de4eb00b6ad6c071a9047ac5e9a66490ade06781a6d4cba590b2d1737f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554020} {:answer/id #uuid "8d418708-fb94-5886-83dc-9a0b98948c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b040d194ecd2e1689402c529eb251cea42dd01dfa8126f0a5117423c72878621.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554166} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9b87e-ae0c-5c29-8c91-0aaa91050925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/066d0669762137e2e014c2b76c941bdf7812c2d537ae1c03e0ae89a1d7107dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554306} {:answer/id #uuid "d12b49a3-f19f-50d3-928c-2490a6f014e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc808580cd0a7dfee4d03a2357d278076650da7acdbe0b963c944ec1338ca66c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6d9b87e-ae0c-5c29-8c91-0aaa91050925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/066d0669762137e2e014c2b76c941bdf7812c2d537ae1c03e0ae89a1d7107dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554306} :answer-field/id #uuid "41892c52-a16d-5548-87d4-6b57bd72c9f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d4fa4d9-0aaf-5089-9a38-77f84d423e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-112005" :question/problem "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -9 & 18 \\\\  &  & 2 & 0 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x-3)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0-3)(0+3) \\\\  & =(-2)(-3)(3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=18,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/06/066d0669762137e2e014c2b76c941bdf7812c2d537ae1c03e0ae89a1d7107dc3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38c7027c-8dc4-5d3a-9a96-5d33d3b8c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0634f6ca-c685-51dd-9320-d4639074b25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06e69e393a48794262d45233bdd0df8f69f2b9ab8a3e9acee5aed3280db9f503.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553880} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8511bf-77e3-5154-87db-8a803f247e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/86/86e4193b55ecabe36aba0dff3624e19a2b89961844a5645817dbfa563129baa7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554022} {:answer/id #uuid "6b377b9a-6945-5b63-b4e3-ee2b2f7a2965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d4/d4733614738a1c241498c092812616e128de5ab4f0e5dd7bd562c219819c14f0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554079} {:answer/id #uuid "d67c0327-7eba-58f1-a5d7-dd85c3ed0c30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48af95d7988c25c5513a60a3962d57ed9a9ee98a966316cb1def1cece96be0d7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554336} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6821e9-999b-5770-954a-4adce57b70ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/87/8781ae0ba7463d56326eb9144c9013ad8811aaea05430295dd7687a454df8ab3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0634f6ca-c685-51dd-9320-d4639074b25b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/06e69e393a48794262d45233bdd0df8f69f2b9ab8a3e9acee5aed3280db9f503.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553880} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd7c1bc3-c7e4-5e0a-971c-0dbc11e064c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60969677-514c-5daf-874b-85d15a063d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112010" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)=x\\cdot x\\cdot (-x)+⋯=-{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(0+2)(2-0) \\\\  & =(4)(2)(2) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,-2,2$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=16,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/06/06e69e393a48794262d45233bdd0df8f69f2b9ab8a3e9acee5aed3280db9f503.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e03f532e-bf46-5933-baf3-b59e42b7cc67" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ff338d-9db1-5cd4-b152-119d52099aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ab/ab99fa53c745af397e67db63b3d6aa892b66b13cd31dcf19503a01e2a8c2fc91.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553971} {:answer/id #uuid "50b3e54f-5a19-5942-97c8-1a0fef6ae694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/2777c31a4504d09e65dd8a9248d8e474146fb50af9723557795105bb4de186be.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554026} {:answer/id #uuid "64595169-25de-552b-b205-b88be4528817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e41620d89bb720602c0271428847e05a6655b8ff1e037c0b408972ceaf252cec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554066} {:answer/id #uuid "734d4893-fde1-5b0c-b618-e1be6f8a10b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac4b0b9c322516609db9bf212eece54f7c2252fbf195c803b827480b0e083c68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554102} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dabf32-5011-5a39-8497-86b98fa63381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5e9bdc79fdd7462a38ba8ffeceef400b8ead638de44d622dc85324ac8cd9c4c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64595169-25de-552b-b205-b88be4528817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e4/e41620d89bb720602c0271428847e05a6655b8ff1e037c0b408972ceaf252cec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554066} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed008ef5-e047-5355-bc29-75fe453b742b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f1e2e562-7654-5d22-adb4-85fe265b1c2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112024" :question/problem "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x-24,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 & 2 & -24 \\\\  &  & 2 & -14 & 24 \\\\  & -1 & 7 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+7x-12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-7x+12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)(x-3)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+2)(0-3)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(2)(-3)(-4) \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an  $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,3,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-24,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/e4/e41620d89bb720602c0271428847e05a6655b8ff1e037c0b408972ceaf252cec.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e03f532e-bf46-5933-baf3-b59e42b7cc67" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00c7a8ec-6d84-537d-83ed-4ac922c27e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f0/f0df952100b03d5714087a60a063abf1a65f4129d7e32e730b82f45e4c57e862.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553866} {:answer/id #uuid "5c326836-cea1-569c-bfc7-a225df73e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8bd6f9c8cbfd1872871a00d403e3e8980c081b03caac5f2c5cc656161a00cd5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554049} {:answer/id #uuid "89b35751-ed9a-5685-a2ce-b98451f0d447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/15/158da0fb2d74d02a63a6871a3b2e4737f1f73971ad8484e7e0dbd44b129c79eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554158} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc388bc-6015-5f15-8fe6-e538d635b4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/791e8ed21436227851d591e51b9a917a088de2b18e3e872a168f978b9309fbf0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554179} {:answer/id #uuid "e5836ce4-600f-562b-abfb-fcdaead9a3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2d3dcdc478bb34ef381154dffe90815dc512f30c75e615a13a31affeaeb0172e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c326836-cea1-569c-bfc7-a225df73e1de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d8/d8bd6f9c8cbfd1872871a00d403e3e8980c081b03caac5f2c5cc656161a00cd5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554049} :answer-field/id #uuid "110bf320-e38a-558c-a223-21f04cb27190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554438}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4fd339f-d602-5df0-954c-b8f9a8d50336" :question/math-academy-id "q-27983" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+7x+6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 7 & 6 \\\\  &  & 1 & -1 & -6 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)(x+2)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,x=-2,$ and $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-(1)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=6,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/d8/d8bd6f9c8cbfd1872871a00d403e3e8980c081b03caac5f2c5cc656161a00cd5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "314a18f1-a890-5a3b-a8ed-6cf505996b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d92575639887aebfc28a664242f83ed5b78f0dbef3cccaa2526bbf2bbf7f1093.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553958} {:answer/id #uuid "355c354e-9879-5d04-9628-bf8c3af50406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/36/360493feba08788b1fec0eaac0005d04362095a4c9dc073c1e0c6fbde19ea6b3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553965} {:answer/id #uuid "4bcef24d-7b0d-557e-8247-dc9e93e19e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/22aff69e331b9a47dca901612d080e9d4ef8f83d3975276878fc0c1f0d44e220.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554016} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba9e4d3-26b7-534b-a2df-02b961f2dcab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4fc0eae860df4a13f1ca1d63581c50798d48fc4911f6d65ba931b4e52e519a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554047} {:answer/id #uuid "62b295bb-30eb-5877-8cf3-0a74c87d0816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f574e24288b0094defccb791407c74d9c3a5eeb1821b3bcac3bfa88153e69f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62b295bb-30eb-5877-8cf3-0a74c87d0816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f574e24288b0094defccb791407c74d9c3a5eeb1821b3bcac3bfa88153e69f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554062} :answer-field/id #uuid "9336d9ac-e8c3-5844-8e2b-9cb0f75979ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "90f18038-3a01-51b3-920b-b432cec605da" :question/math-academy-id "q-28058" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=-4.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-2)(0-3)(0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(-3)(4)}{6} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=4,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f574e24288b0094defccb791407c74d9c3a5eeb1821b3bcac3bfa88153e69f5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0351d442-e295-5767-b615-4ef20bb63c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/79855a67f1703f5982fe3406ce5db72e1a2af087d94a6e4fe34db9b8f68a85ca.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553872} {:answer/id #uuid "20b71412-ebeb-5e1d-859d-b822ed7d9231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/954b771542e9118f8417e5b5be35803e75d788ea1433c58f6430d985af4a57fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553925} {:answer/id #uuid "477b9f30-5f38-5116-9a22-73330deb5652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/0514909ce208ebf90584d7130705b5eccc0db08596d558dd83467b7cfda925fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554006} {:answer/id #uuid "642eb021-cd1a-5d4d-843e-e1a50d704511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/38/38f1108c6b4e04f1d505bc8234d97bb57ccf49202769c8d9624e950fdeb2dcd4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554065} {:answer/id #uuid "7a82002a-f169-572c-aa0c-f1dda8a63cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49ce31e582c6965da24d40167bfee7890dc1b898bcf1b783b010b831287d80da.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "477b9f30-5f38-5116-9a22-73330deb5652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/05/0514909ce208ebf90584d7130705b5eccc0db08596d558dd83467b7cfda925fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554006} :answer-field/id #uuid "d413ff44-236b-5252-8dd6-4240371ec92d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554520}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "93eea4d8-b061-5509-a1b0-2eaeace6d46a" :question/math-academy-id "q-28059" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)(x-3)(x+5)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)(x-3)(x+5)=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-1$, $x=3$ and $x=-5.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+1)(0-3)(0+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(-3)(5)}{3} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-5,-1,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-5,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/05/0514909ce208ebf90584d7130705b5eccc0db08596d558dd83467b7cfda925fa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b987328-b96c-54e5-b3ef-a8481771cb8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/ca6e63fa7484bb39b42e05b1843d456da7273140a41f1e56698d86dcbb442f13.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113041} {:answer/id #uuid "edaed251-df82-5f8d-91f1-16b4b286bddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0dea0dbddc4ed16a26a917e9e0ae589a78e5fb38b80fc95972ed6bae8fc39098.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113042} {:answer/id #uuid "26debacb-40a4-5f93-a67b-614127eba273" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a420da3798b127b1afccdefbc4c45087da673b92ed4465cd0a9f8a245d8dd454.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113043} {:answer/id #uuid "3170dcc9-810e-56c9-8215-3b566022480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bc52ee56cc821a2d498ebee872c620a61c527f0967b3cb6de985bd10ffce97a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113044} {:answer/id #uuid "58d0820b-8c13-59c3-b73d-d1d3e708e115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a138a79ebd4632740ba5c9556a48086d304c5201ab1ffec03e9a23f3074f206.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3170dcc9-810e-56c9-8215-3b566022480c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8b/8bc52ee56cc821a2d498ebee872c620a61c527f0967b3cb6de985bd10ffce97a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113044} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef212097-2d0c-5ec5-a42a-ffbe33b2b0dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4eaa158c-d63a-514a-919f-df3efa8bad33" :question/math-academy-id "q-28331" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)?$ \n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}(x-1)(x+2)(x+3)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=1,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-1)(0+2)(0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)(2)(3)}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-2,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/8b/8bc52ee56cc821a2d498ebee872c620a61c527f0967b3cb6de985bd10ffce97a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115287 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eea927d4-f52d-5583-879f-d70a0f40a1e0" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11204ec8-a1ac-5e56-a7ec-6a793c6d9b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8e3dce54c5c4131d0bd68ed9c3481ccaa26ca93d799795941d4c6f303b0a6a8b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553904} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3c37c0-c74e-59d8-b081-edd1a9328982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/caeaca305217c61f2715c8594749158b80b9e25ed0c56dfc4a7a3272f8903038.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554114} {:answer/id #uuid "88894f92-5d4b-5479-b78f-1c05f2fc170a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/07/078cb44dc7e14390813616440e67a23c8b77e51eab0cd58c4080f2c5d54f3e17.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554153} {:answer/id #uuid "db59d555-bcb7-53a2-b399-e6a3acaf9d4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6a/6a925943c2286b3390f219e7a9fbb76ad52fa4c8bd3a1c42f374a1c270dcc6f5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554347} {:answer/id #uuid "ea971ece-bb53-5131-aea0-2ba8817c511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e27e18b8e79c71dcc888e62c4472723b44b1b4fa0367e124313f95bc08ef70fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554380}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea971ece-bb53-5131-aea0-2ba8817c511d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e27e18b8e79c71dcc888e62c4472723b44b1b4fa0367e124313f95bc08ef70fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554380} :answer-field/id #uuid "35457a0d-0c61-5711-94e0-bb380f8efb12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dae1615e-910b-570a-9c8d-abe635df0b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-28492" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-1,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+1)(0+2)(0-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(2)(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,5$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-2,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/e2/e27e18b8e79c71dcc888e62c4472723b44b1b4fa0367e124313f95bc08ef70fa.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38c7027c-8dc4-5d3a-9a96-5d33d3b8c1c7" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Factored Cubic Curve with Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a93e68d-5525-5c5c-a5c0-40a3006df82b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10ac49b1571ab0c058ae72991382cf90710b8bc4e7f973b6152d7176df4841f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553919} {:answer/id #uuid "25a5e0b4-78f0-519c-aaa8-487ec25837a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/2852cbbe5f7e3e9dfb02968b92d2d3f1eca405b972dc716167375979958645e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553936} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5cec13-7b2b-54b0-acd9-66e2a154b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48a836ec46ede6f53d6a608239edb89aa33d083d265dada32ff887871ff98f1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554017} {:answer/id #uuid "9ea388b8-b1ee-5301-9987-ad4ecee46eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2c/2c5a0229cafec77667adf228886198d6f551704fd6bb6a67beb4f702bca924b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554218} {:answer/id #uuid "e47e4051-8811-5cb7-9dc4-b1517ded5708" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/210fa8caf7365154cb85e3f9d23d400a0e69c34920ffc49129abdf4e79b86c27.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25a5e0b4-78f0-519c-aaa8-487ec25837a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/28/2852cbbe5f7e3e9dfb02968b92d2d3f1eca405b972dc716167375979958645e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553936} :answer-field/id #uuid "76de3f59-19a0-578d-bd08-9da8e8b2dd4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1bca3374-bd66-579c-b0ab-3963f1fce03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-28501" :question/problem "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{6}(x+3)(x-2)(x-3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}(x+3)(x-2)(x-3)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-3,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0+3)(0-2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3)(-2)(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/28/2852cbbe5f7e3e9dfb02968b92d2d3f1eca405b972dc716167375979958645e4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186554544 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115288 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5914ea8b-a7f5-5fe0-8060-34cb47f5d401" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing an Expanded Cubic Curve with Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f473e41-b55f-50e2-81be-a6ebc3c9f999" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/be/be78ab23063af0f890931c2f40b920495012a8234571e5858a571710a1a91b98.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554091} {:answer/id #uuid "9d508d81-98c0-536f-89eb-9ec315f7b71e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a3215ae0019c93a9e189bb2ff3d1019c3d8ab803b0a754b741671da06474e6e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554215} {:answer/id #uuid "bf1cfb03-667f-5afb-9c47-360e2bed892f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/39/39b3430d7a1d465b2f9e5c39d0106c60ad8b333919c24bb5bbb318866ba07408.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554285} {:answer/id #uuid "bff52a36-ae0d-513f-bea3-ebe09da40039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40b0fc54d72820b2893dba5160b4e5f1c70bf7b63aebd63eb3d5ea0ad078dcef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554288} {:answer/id #uuid "d53366b5-6acf-5a37-96bf-fb9f8a1a7546" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e64162a9f7c30293c23a08bcbfbddabe3d3da54a98e98181c62abe372448e700.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff52a36-ae0d-513f-bea3-ebe09da40039" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/40/40b0fc54d72820b2893dba5160b4e5f1c70bf7b63aebd63eb3d5ea0ad078dcef.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554288} :answer-field/id #uuid "f36e70e9-2a1f-5781-8df0-6ffb61fd4403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186554532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "17f76fde-fb9f-52ba-9f93-d56954798f7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-73907" :question/problem "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & -1 & -3 \\\\  &  & -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+1)(0-1)(0+3) \\\\  & =(1)(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-1,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/40/40b0fc54d72820b2893dba5160b4e5f1c70bf7b63aebd63eb3d5ea0ad078dcef.png)"}]}
